{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 28, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.2-3b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a prince of the County of Tripoli in the 12th century. He was the son of Bohemond I and the brother of Bohemond II Pons and Amalric I. Born around 1150 he became the Count of Tripoli in 1166.\n\n**Early Life and Reign**\nRaymond was born to Bohemond I and Matilda of Edessa. His early life is not well documented however it is known that he received a good education and was trained in the art of war. He became the Count of Tripoli in 1166 upon the death of his brother Bohemond II Pons. Raymond was a skilled military leader and was able to defend the County of Tripoli against the forces of the Fatimid Caliphate.\n\n**The Crusader States**\nDuring his reign Raymond III was a key figure in the Crusader states. He was a member of the Holy Sepulchre and the Knights Templar. He also played a role in the Battle of Montgisard in 1177. Raymond was able to maintain the County of Tripoli as an independent state despite being surrounded by the Muslim forces of the Fatimid Caliphate.\n\n**Relationship with the Kingdom of Jerusalem**\nRaymond III had a complex relationship with the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was a member of the royal family and was related to the King of Jerusalem however he often found himself at odds with the ruling monarch. In 1177 Raymond was forced to ally with the Kingdom of Jerusalem against the forces of the Fatimid Caliphate. However he was also known to have had dealings with the Muslim forces of the Ayyubid dynasty.\n\nRaymond III Count of Tripoli died in 1172. He was succeeded by his brother Amalric I who went on to become King of Jerusalem. The County of Tripoli remained an independent state until it was conquered by the Mamluks in 1289.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.0013606823049485683}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.000128499363199807